<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kushan Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kushan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kushan Empire was a state founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> that expanded into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the first centuries CE. Its control of trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the Roman world made it wealthy, and it patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Gandharan art. Its decline left a political space in northern India in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> later rose.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kushan-empire.jpg" alt="Kushan Empire" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: Schwartzberg Atlas, v., p. 145. Map g · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kushan_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Chinese sources describe the Kushans as one of five aristocratic tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>. Many scholars believe the Yuezhi were an Indo-European people, and some suggest a Tocharian origin, while others propose an Iranic Saka origin. The Records of the Great Historian and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> place them in the grasslands of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> beheaded their king and forced them west between 176 and 160 BCE.</p><p>Traces of the Kushans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Sogdiana date to the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE, where they displaced the Sakas and built fortresses on the ruins of Hellenistic cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a>. The earliest documented ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraios">Heraios</a>, called himself a tyrant in Greek on his coins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a> later welded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> tribes into a tight confederation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kushan rulers are recorded for about three centuries, from around 30 to around 375 CE. Vima Takto expanded the empire into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vima-kadphises">Vima Kadphises</a> added conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. Kanishka the Great, the fourth king, ruled a huge territory from his accession. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a> it reached south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and Kundina and east beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huvishka">Huvishka</a>'s rule was a period of retrenchment and consolidation, in which he devoted effort to controlling the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i">Vasudeva I</a> was the last of the Great Kushans, and the end of his rule coincides with the Sasanian invasion as far as north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The empire fragmented into semi-independent kingdoms in the 3rd century, which fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, who established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushano-sasanian-kingdom">Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom</a>. In the 4th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> pressed from the east, and the last Kushan and Kushano-Sasanian kingdoms were overwhelmed by invaders from the north, the Kidarites and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kushans initially used Greek for administration, then adopted the Eastern Iranian Bactrian language, written in an adapted Greek script. Their gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins carried more than thirty gods, drawn from Iranian, Greek and Indian worlds, together with images of kings and the Buddha. Kushan rulers were patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> is remembered in Buddhist tradition for convening a great council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Between the mid-1st and mid-3rd century, Buddhism, patronised by the Kushans, spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and other Asian countries along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p><p>The art of Gandhara, at the crossroads of Kushan power, developed Greco-Buddhist traditions. The Kushans also had diplomatic contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in the 1st and 2nd centuries they collaborated militarily with the Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a>, though in 86 they marched against him and were defeated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kushan Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Yuezhi later founded the <b>Kushan Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gupta Empire arose in the Ganges plain in a period of political fragmentation that followed the decline of the <b>Kushan Empire</b> in the northwest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The main rivals, however, were the Western Satraps, Saka rulers of Gujarat, Malwa and western India who were probably at first subordinate to the <b>Kushan Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>This migration of the Yuezhi set off a series of population movements that ended with their settlement in Bactria and the later rise of the <b>Kushan Empire</b>, one of the great crossroads states of Eurasian history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>With his rise the Yuezhi state became the <b>Kushan Empire</b>, which under his successors extended deep into northern India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It flourished under the <b>Kushan Empire</b>, whose territories in Gandhara and Bactria produced a Buddhist art shaped by Greek and Roman models, and it reached China by the 1st century CE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
